ompensation of $22K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 1\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (10/10).","financial_summary":["Credit Acceptance Corporation is a mid-cap financial services business with a $5.4B market cap on $2.3B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +8% over the past three years and +11% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 39% against a 11% cost of capital \u2014 a 28-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat.","The business throws off $1.1B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 45 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 2.49\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and thin 2.2\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Management returns a meaningful 13.1% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 38% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and within its sector the business captures 1.7\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.3) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Very elevated short interest (31.7% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against the business","sev":"red","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Operating margins 